\documentclass[11pt]{report}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Estuary Ecology Monitoring: Annual Report}
\author{P.~Quillen and R.~Abara}
\date{2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\tableofcontents

\chapter{Survey Area}
The lower estuary spans eleven kilometers from the highway bridge to
the river mouth.
\section{Geomorphology}
Meander scars mark the old channel on both banks, and the present
channel migrates a full bank width per decade.
\section{Hydrology}
Tides push brackish water four kilometers upstream on spring cycles,
which sets where each survey station sits.

\chapter{Field Campaigns}
Crews sample monthly from April to October, plus one winter campaign if
ice allows.
\section{Sampling Protocol}
Each station yields three water grabs and two sediment cores, labeled
before the boat moves.
\section{Quality Control}
Field crews run one duplicate grab per station per day and log every
deviation on the custody sheet.

\chapter{Laboratory Analysis}
Shore laboratory work starts within six hours of the boat landing.
\subsection{Chain of Custody}
Custody sheets travel with the coolers and are countersigned at every
hand-off.
A sample without a complete custody chain is logged but excluded from
the annual statistics.
\section{Nutrient Assays}
Nitrate and phosphate assays run in duplicate on the autoanalyzer, with
a calibration curve every twelve samples.

\chapter{Long-term Trends}
The trends chapter pools eleven years of station records.
\section{Trend Detection}
A Mann--Kendall screen flags monotonic trends, and a seasonal version
handles the strong spring signal.
\section{Quality Control}
Quarterly review re-runs five percent of archived assays to keep old
and new data comparable.

\end{document}

